  "definition": {
   "definition": "M1, or narrow money, is the most liquid measure of money supply: M1 = currency with the public + demand deposits with banks + 'other' deposits with the Reserve Bank. It is published alongside M2 and M3 in RBI's 'Money Stock: Components and Sources' (item 6 of the Weekly Statistical Supplement) and in the Monthly Bulletin. M1 tracks transactions demand for money — it excludes time deposits, so it moves with spending and cash preference rather than savings behaviour, which is why it is read together with M3.",
